//
// A NULLABLE HOLE INTO A NON-NULL PARAMETER WARNS (`@FR-F-Target-Kind`, crossed with
// `@FR-N-Store`).  Nullability is not a hole KIND — a `text?` hole is a `text` hole and an
// `integer?` hole an `integer` one — so whether a null may arrive is the hole method's own
// parameter type to say (the compiler's comment: "`v: text?` takes both, `v: text` takes only
// the non-null one").  Storing a `τ?` into a non-null parameter is `(N-Store)`'s warning when
// the call is written by hand; the call the template writes owes the same warning.
//
// ⚠ DEFECT (reported by the rule walk, 2026-10-18): the template path emits no warning.
// `q: Q = "{ni}{tn}"` with `ni: integer? = null`, `tn: text? = null` and non-null
// `hole_int(v: integer)` / `hole_text(v: text)` hands both nulls in and logs `I(null)T(null)`
// with no diagnostic, while `q.hole_int(ni)` / `q.hole_text(tn)` written by hand each warn
// "a nullable `integer?` is stored into parameter 2 of `hole_int` of the non-null type".
//
// @IGNORE — the defect above: the template's hole call skips the (N-Store) warning.
// @falsified-at: none — the expected warnings do not exist on any build yet; this cell is the
//   receipt the fix must turn green.
// CHANNEL: expect — the two warnings.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: today the file runs warning-free and logs `I(null)T(null)`.
// HOLDS: the logged values (the null still arrives; the warning is what is owed).

struct Q { const log: vector<text> }

fn lit(self: Q, s: text) { self.log += ["L({s})"]; }
fn hole_int(self: Q, v: integer) { self.log += ["I({v})"]; }
fn hole_text(self: Q, v: text) { self.log += ["T({v})"]; }

// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into parameter 2 of `hole_int`
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `text?` is stored into parameter 2 of `hole_text`
fn test_a_null_into_a_non_null_hole() {
  ni: integer? = null;
  tn: text? = null;
  q: Q = "{ni}{tn}";
  assert(len(q.log) == 2, "{len(q.log)}");
}
